From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] samsung-sit: keep several requests outstanding, as stock does

The CP answers out of order and will sit on a finished reply until the AP
writes again. Measured on hardware, with the reply arriving relative to our
own next write:

    0x0d17   answer held 10332ms, landed   1ms after our next write
    0x0600   answer held 10456ms           (r55, under the timeout: survived)
    0x0600   answer held 30000ms, landed   3ms after our timeout fired

One command at a time cannot survive that: the write that would release the
reply is the one we would only make once we had it, so the reply lands when
our own timeout fires and is then dropped as stale. A boot showed seven
SETUP_DATA_CALL replies and two SET_PREFERRED_DATA_MODEM replies discarded
that way, and a dropped SETUP_DATA_CALL reply is a context the CP granted
and we then leaked -- which is also what made provisioning loop forever
rather than fail.

Stock does not meet any of this. It keeps up to nine requests outstanding
on one channel and five on the other, 14% of its frames have more than one
in flight, and across a whole boot it is never silent for more than 124ms
with a request outstanding. We were at exactly one, always.

So match it: write each request immediately, keep the outstanding ones in a
table keyed by the token the CP echoes, and match replies by that token
whenever they arrive. Replies are picked up in parse_unsolicited(), which
MMPortSerial calls for every inbound buffer whether or not it has a command
of its own in flight, so a reply is never late and the port's own response
parsing is no longer used. Each request keeps its own timeout, and a reply
for nothing outstanding is reported rather than silently eaten.

Frames are written one per write, never two together and never part of one:
the ring is message-oriented and both have broken it before. A blocked
transport parks the frame at the head of the queue so order is kept.

The queued-commands test's peer read one frame per read, which only held
while writes were serialised; with both requests outstanding they arrive in
one read on a SOCK_STREAM socket, exactly as on the sit-proxy socket, so it
now frames by the header length like the port itself does.

